{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION TO MY OWN PLAN: the alert store CANNOT settle AC1 for these cells\n\nMy earlier comment offered three routes and said option 1 (read the `bridge_alerts` store inside the\nfailing cell) was the cheapest, with option 2 (a raw-REST probe) worth doing first. **Option 1 does\nnot work here at all**, and recommending it would have built exactly the vacuous oracle this epic\nkeeps producing.\n\n### The call graph, traced\n\n`record_parent_divergence` — the write path I was counting on — is invoked from **exactly one\nplace**: `dispatch_one._update_one`, wrapping the `client.set_parent` call the RECONCILER'S OWN\nDISPATCH LOOP makes during a differ-driven pass (`dispatch_one.py:551-601`).\n\nBoth live sub-task cells call `dc_transport.set_parent(child, second)` **DIRECTLY**, not through a\npass. Their own docstrings say why: the outbound emit gate (bug 8b25) never emits a parent-field\nmutation for a non-epic parent, so the pass-level round-trip is structurally unreachable on DC and\nthe cells deliberately assert against the transport instead.\n\n**So `record_parent_divergence` is never invoked by either cell, whatever DC does.** Reading\n`bridge_alerts` afterwards returns `[]` whether DC errored, silently ignored the write, or the field\nactually moved. A \"no alert\" reading would prove only that the mutation bypassed the reconciler —\nprecisely the \"silence is not a signal\" mistake I corrected once already on 39c1, reappearing one\nlevel down.\n\n### What this changes\n\n**Option 2 (raw REST probe) is not merely cheaper — it is the ONLY route that can settle AC1 for\nthese two cells**, because it depends on no rebar code path being on the route. And it needs no new\nplumbing: the `dc_request` fixture already supports `method=\"PUT\"` with a `payload`\n(`conftest.py:81-117`).\n\nConcretely, in `test_outbound_set_subtask_parent_round_trips` (and the inbound twin at :1301): before\nthe existing `set_parent` call, issue a direct `PUT fields.parent` and capture `(status, body)`. A\n2xx with an unchanged read-back is **accept-and-ignore**; a 4xx is **rejection**. That is AC1's\nquestion, answered with the raw response, independent of rebar.\n\nWorth pairing with the two cheap falsifiers recorded on [rebar:37e7-d751-0042-4b94]: read\n`/rest/api/2/issue/{subtask}/editmeta` for the operations `parent` actually exposes, and compare the\n`update`-verb form's error to the `fields` form's.\n\n### The alert-store helper is still worth building — for [rebar:18a5-2bd8-3e56-4bd8], not here\n\nA designed `assert_bridge_alert_for_mutation(repo, cp, ...)` helper in `_dc_support.py` (510 LOC, ~290\nof headroom) does have a real payoff on 18a5, whose cell DOES run a real pass and therefore does\nreach `record_backstop_failure`. Two properties matter and are worth stating so a future\nimplementation keeps them:\n\n * **It takes the pass's `CompletedProcess` as a REQUIRED argument** and asserts `returncode == 0`\n   and no traceback BEFORE interpreting the store. Without that, an absent alert directory is\n   indistinguishable from a clean pass that wrote nothing — `alert_store.append` creates the\n   directory only when it first writes (`alert_store.py:117-119`), so filesystem state alone cannot\n   tell \"DC succeeded\" from \"nobody looked\".\n * **On 18a5's cell it is diagnostic either way**: an alert found surfaces the specific swallowed\n   exception (that ticket's open question); NO alert on a provably-clean pass is a *stronger* result\n   — it would mean the create was never attempted, moving 18a5 from stage 3 back to stage 1/2."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785792614089161001,"uuid":"3af1bf02-5b6b-478c-a204-3c28b3ef34b5"}